.TH GETPWENT_R 3
.SH NAME
getpwent_r, fgetpwent_r \- reentrant enumeration of the password database
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <pwd.h>
.PP
.BI "int getpwent_r(struct passwd *" pwbuf ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ,
.BI "struct passwd **" result ");"
.br
.BI "int fgetpwent_r(FILE *" stream ", struct passwd *" pwbuf ,
.BI "char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", struct passwd **" result ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the reentrant equivalents of
.BR getpwent (3)
and
.BR fgetpwent (3).
Each reads one password-database entry into the caller-supplied
.I pwbuf
structure, whose string members point into the caller-supplied scratch buffer
.I buf
(length
.IR buflen ),
and sets
.I *result
to
.I pwbuf
(or NULL at end of file).
.TP
.B getpwent_r
returns the next entry from the internal stream positioned by
.BR setpwent (3)/ endpwent (3).
.TP
.B fgetpwent_r
reads the next entry from an already-open
.I stream
in
.IR /etc/passwd
format.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Return 0 on success.  At end of file
.B fgetpwent_r
returns ENOENT and
.I *result
is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ERANGE
.I buf
was too small to hold the entry.
.TP
.B ENOENT
End of file (no more entries).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getpwent (3),
.BR getpwnam_r (3),
.BR getgrent_r (3),
.BR passwd (5)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX specifies
.BR getpwnam_r ;
.B getpwent_r
and
.B fgetpwent_r
are GNU extensions.
